  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1211 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nHigh pressure stretched north to south over the eastern Plains\ncontinues to control regional weather through Friday. HRRR and \nRAP model soundings display a column dominated by subsidence and\ndry low-level air, with surface dew points holding in the 60s.\n \nTemperatures remain seasonably mild. Highs today and Friday \nreach the low-mid 80s under mostly sunny skies. Surface winds \nwill be light through Friday with the ridge axis nearby. As the\nhigh slides east toward the Mid- Atlantic on Saturday, low- \nlevel flow transitions to the south- southeast, initiating warm \nair advection. Afternoon highs on Saturday warm into the mid \n80s, while overnight lows drop comfortably to around 60 the next\ncouple nights.\n\nA synoptic pattern shift occurs Sunday into early next week as \nan expansive 500 mb ridge amplifies over the central Plains and\nexpands into the Midwest. High confidence exists regarding the \nsubstantial temperature surge, while lower confidence surrounds \nthe southern extent of convective tracks riding the ridge \nperimeter.\n\nNotable model disagreements emerge regarding ridge amplitude \nand structure as we head through the early to mid portions of\nnext week. More amplified solutions keep the storm track to our\nnorth, anchoring the primary track of active weather across \nMinnesota, Wisconsin, and far northern Illinois. Meanwhile, \nmodel solutions favoring a flatter ridge would bring the \nsouthern edge of the \"ring of fire\" into parts of central IL. \nThe forecast relies on the NBM, which favors the amplified \nsolution and lower PoPs, mainly affecting the far northern CWA\nwith slight chance levels (20% or less) through mid next week. \n\nThermal advection ramps up under the ridge, with 850 mb \ntemperatures climbing near +25 C. NBM guidance projects highs \nreaching the lower 90s Sunday, then lower to mid 90s Monday \nthrough Wednesday. Accompanied by dew points in the upper 60s \nto lower 70s, peak heat index values range between 100-105 F. \nClimatologically, the ECMWF Extreme Forecast Index (EFI) \nhighlights this heat wave with maximum temperature EFI values \nbetween 0.70 and 0.85 across central Illinois from Monday \nthrough Wednesday, signaling an unusually hot airmass for late \nAugust to early September. The Climate Prediction Center (CPC) \n6-10 Day Outlook reinforces this trend, leaning heavily toward \nabove- normal temperatures across the region with a 70 to 80 \npercent probability Sept. 1-5.",
